+# browser-quality-gates — brief
+Goal:            Turn browser compatibility, accessibility, and visual stability into explicit local and CI quality gates: declare the supported browser policy; retain the full existing Chromium Playwright suite; add a bounded Firefox/WebKit journey matrix; add automated WCAG-oriented accessibility checks over representative public, authenticated, dense, and degraded states; add deterministic screenshot baselines for a small set of high-risk responsive/theme states; and expose clear Nx/npm targets for checking versus deliberately updating baselines. Preserve application runtime behavior, API behavior, scoring, motion design, and trader-visible UI.
+Decision impact: N/A — frontend delivery correctness. Success is observed when a real browser-engine incompatibility, serious/critical accessibility regression, or approved visual baseline drift fails a named local command with actionable artifacts, while identical reruns remain byte/stability-clean and the existing full Chromium suite does not multiply blindly across every engine.
+Feasibility:     pass — code-grounded 2026-08-23: `apps/dashboard-e2e` already uses Playwright 1.62.1 through Nx and runs 52 Chromium tests across five routes, responsive bands, both color schemes, authenticated states, and dense fixtures; its config explicitly records Firefox/WebKit as the deferred follow-on. No first-party Browserslist policy, axe Playwright integration, or Playwright screenshot baseline exists. The council must lock the supported browser promise, which journeys run on all engines, the accessibility severity/scope boundary, deterministic visual-fixture rules, baseline review/update authority, and what remains locally verifiable while GitHub-hosted runners are unavailable.
+Effort:          M
+Invariant watch: structural-over-prose-enforcement; theme-token-discipline; single-motion-moment; absent-is-not-none; live-vs-static-isolation; loss-free-durable-migration; operator-vs-trader-path-separation; cited-code-is-verified-not-assumed
+Context tags:    frontend,testing,ci,accessibility,design-system,performance,invariants
+Source:          owner-directed next implementation after the supply-chain hosted-evidence pause; previously queued as `browser-accessibility-visual-matrix` in `supply-chain-automation/BRIEF.md`; `OPEN_THREADS.md` §8 records Firefox/WebKit as the deliberately separate Playwright follow-on.
+Considered:      browser-quality-gates (CHOSEN — owner-directed; closes a documented browser/UI verification gap with observable, mutation-testable gates) · capacity-baseline (parked, not dropped — production-maturity item #5 remains next in that program, but the owner explicitly redirected while hosted supply-chain evidence is unavailable) · nx-affected-remote-cache-sync (queued after browser coverage; cache trust/spend is a separate operational decision) · tanstack-query-http-server-state (queued after build/test foundations; HTTP server state only, no global-state rewrite) · msw-api-fixtures (queued behind the query boundary; do not introduce a second fixture authority during this feature) · telemetry-slos (parked in the existing production-maturity sequence) · risk-workflow-decomposition (parked; refactor only under the stronger browser gates) · react-compiler-pilot (parked last; bounded pilot, not a repository-wide switch) · Storybook/Chromatic/Percy (culled for this slice: hosted service/tooling overlap and ongoing cost are unnecessary for a small Playwright-native baseline) · Motion/GSAP, a second charting library, Redux/Zustand, Axios, GraphQL/tRPC, Tailwind/shadcn/Radix (culled: no measured capability gap relevant to this feature).
+
+Explicit non-goals: changing product UI/copy/motion; modifying backend/API/runtime code; multiplying all 52 tests across three engines; treating automated axe checks as a complete manual accessibility audit; accepting new screenshots without an explicit review command; claiming hosted-browser evidence while GitHub Actions runners/billing are unavailable.
